error":false,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"```json\n{\"blind_id\":\"ST064\",\"coverage\":[{\"point\":1,\"credit\":1,\"reason\":\"States the Rule 6c-11(a)(1) AP definition in full: a member or participant of an SEC-registered clearing agency under Section 17A, with a written agreement with the ETF or one of its service providers allowing it to place creation/redemption orders.\"},{\"point\":2,\"credit\":0,\"reason\":\"Says it lacks the reviewed forms and gives only a generic list of industry parties (trust, distributor, AP, sometimes TA/custodian). It does not identify the distributor and AP as main counterparties, the transfer agent's countersignature or acceptance, or the trust's third-party beneficiary role.\"},{\"point\":3,\"credit\":0.5,\"reason\":\"Names NSCC (CNS) and/or DTC as the practical clearing routes, which partly covers the NSCC-netting versus DTC-outside-the-process distinction. It does not say that AP eligibility is not the same as being a market maker.\"}],\"central_error\":null,\"local_error\":null,\"grade\":\"partial\",\"rationale\":\"Required coverage is 1.5 of 3, which meets the half threshold, and core requested facts are supplied. There is no central error: the answer does not deny the clearing or written-agreement requirements, and it does not explicitly call the trust the main signatory. The generic 'commonly involve the trust/ETF' wording is hedged and is not a central false statement. No material local falsehood was found.\"}\n```","ttft_ms":4091,"type":"result","duration_ms":8061,"uuid":"e7468e3f-d6b3-4b32-9399-2b2c14cbffa7","ttft_stream_ms":1506,"time_to_request_ms":62,"first_content_frame_ms":1507,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
